          <a href="#switch" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Switching Between Them</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every disappointing stone sealing job we have looked at comes down to the same thing: a film-forming product on a surface that needed to breathe, or a penetrating product on a surface someone wanted to look glossy.
        </p>

        <div id="how" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Each One Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            An impregnating sealer — also sold as a penetrating sealer — carries an active repellent into the stone's pore structure, where it lines the pore walls and then the carrier evaporates away. Nothing is left on the surface. Water and oil landing on the stone find the capillaries already occupied and sit on top instead of wicking in, which is the whole mechanism.
          </p>

          <p>
            A topical sealer stays on the surface. It cures into a continuous film — acrylic, urethane or wax depending on the product — that physically separates the stone from whatever lands on it. It is the same principle as a varnish on wood, and it brings the same advantages and the same eventual problems.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">An impregnator changes the stone. A topical sealer covers it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Once you can see it that way, most of the decision makes itself. Anything that gets walked on, cut on or cleaned daily will wear a film off. Anything that needs to look wet or glossy cannot get there without one.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The active chemistry differs too. Quality impregnators use fluoropolymers, which repel oil as well as water — the property that matters in a kitchen. Cheaper ones use silanes or siloxanes, which handle water well and oil poorly. It is the same chemistry family used on concrete, and the same trade-off we set out in our guide to <a href='/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/'>silane versus siloxane sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither type makes stone stain-proof. An impregnator buys you time — minutes to hours instead of seconds — to wipe a spill up before it marks. A topical film keeps the spill off entirely while the film is intact, which on a working surface is measured in months rather than years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost tracks that difference more closely than brand does. A quality fluoropolymer impregnator runs 25 to 60 dollars for a bottle that will do several kitchens, because the active content is small and the carrier does the work. Topical sealers are cheaper per bottle and cover less, and they have to be bought again far more often, so the running cost of a film finish is always the higher of the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="look" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Each Does to the Look
          </h2>

          <p>
            A correctly applied impregnating sealer is invisible. The stone looks exactly as it did before, with the same sheen and the same colour. If a countertop looks different after sealing with an impregnator, something went wrong — usually product left to dry on the surface rather than being buffed off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Topical sealers change everything. They add gloss, deepen colour and give the wet look that a lot of people actually want on slate, flagstone and travertine. On a patio or an entryway floor that enhancement can be the entire point of sealing, and no penetrating product will ever deliver it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Impregnating</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Topical</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No change</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gloss or satin, deepened colour</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Slip resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unchanged</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduced when wet</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shows application errors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lap marks, streaks, bubbles</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical life indoors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical life outdoors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-24 months</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Removable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with stripper</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The slip line in that table is not a footnote. A glossy topical film on a stone floor, a pool surround or an entryway is genuinely slippery when wet, and it needs a slip additive broadcast into the final coat. That is standard practice in the trade and routinely skipped by homeowners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Application difficulty follows from the same physics. An impregnator applied slightly unevenly leaves no evidence, because the excess is buffed away and what matters happened inside the stone. A topical sealer records every lap mark, roller line, air bubble and thin patch permanently in the film, which is why a first-time film application on a large floor so often ends in stripping it back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Each One Fails
          </h2>

          <p>
            Topical sealers fail visibly and on a schedule you can watch. Traffic abrades the film, UV breaks it down outdoors, and cleaning wears it thinner. The sequence runs from dulling to patchiness to flaking, and the flaking stage looks considerably worse than unsealed stone ever did. Six months to three years is the range, depending on where it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impregnators fail invisibly. The repellent in the pores degrades, the stone starts taking on water again, and nothing about the appearance changes to warn you. That is why the water test is the only maintenance tool that matters with a penetrating product — the countertop gives no other signal until something stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one failure mode unique to topical sealers and it is the important one: moisture trapped beneath the film. Stone over a slab, a patio on grade or a floor without a vapour barrier moves water vapour upwards continuously. A film that vapour cannot pass through blisters, whitens and lifts, and no amount of surface care prevents it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impregnators cannot fail that way because they leave the pore structure open to vapour while making it repellent to liquid. That single property is why they dominate every exterior and every below-grade application, and it is the same reasoning behind our recommendations in the <a href='/best-natural-stone-sealer/'>natural stone sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="breathe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Vapour Permeability, and Why It Decides Most Cases
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stone laid on a mortar bed, over a concrete slab or outdoors on grade is always in contact with moisture from below. Ground water rises, slabs wick, and the vapour has to leave somewhere. In an unsealed or impregnated installation it leaves through the stone, which is what stone has done for as long as buildings have existed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put a film over the top and the vapour arrives at a barrier. Pressure builds under the film, and because the bond between a topical sealer and stone is measured in pounds per square inch rather than anything heroic, the film loses. The result is white blushing first, then blisters, then sheets peeling away — usually starting in the areas that get least sun and therefore dry slowest.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same mechanism drives efflorescence, where dissolved salts carried up by that vapour crystallise at the surface. Under a film they crystallise beneath it and push it off; on an impregnated surface they appear on top and can be brushed away. Neither is pleasant, but only one of them destroys the sealing job.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the practical rule is simple: if the stone is in contact with the ground or a slab, or is outdoors, use an impregnator. A topical sealer belongs on stone that is dry on all sides — an indoor floor over a ventilated void, a fireplace surround, a piece of furniture — or on a surface where you accept periodic stripping as part of the deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Surface Takes Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the confusion resolves the moment you name the surface. This is what we would specify without knowing anything else about the installation.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Granite kitchen countertop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Food contact, daily cleaning, hot pans</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Marble countertop or vanity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film wears unevenly and cannot stop etching</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Slate or flagstone patio</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vapour from the ground, freeze-thaw</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Indoor slate floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Topical if the wet look is wanted</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Travertine or limestone floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft, porous, and usually over a slab</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stone shower walls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Constant wetting, vapour must escape</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fireplace surround</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry on all sides, no traffic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pool surround</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impregnating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slip safety and constant wetting</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Countertops are the least ambiguous case on that list. A film on a kitchen worktop meets knives, hot pans, daily detergent and standing water, and it will not survive any of them for long. It also cannot be repaired locally — a scratched film has to be stripped and redone across the whole surface. Impregnators are the only defensible choice, which is why every product in our <a href='/best-granite-sealer/'>granite sealer guide</a> is one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Marble deserves its own note because people expect sealing to fix etching, and it does not. Etching is acid chemically dulling the surface, and an impregnator does not sit on top to stop it while a topical film is too thin and too short-lived to help. What sealing prevents on marble is staining, which is a different problem — our <a href='/best-marble-sealer/'>marble sealer guide</a> separates the two properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="enhance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Colour-Enhancing Sealers: The Middle Ground
          </h2>

          <p>
            Colour-enhancing sealers are impregnators with an added resin that deepens the stone the way water does when it is wet. They give much of the visual effect people want from a topical product while remaining vapour permeable and inside the stone, which makes them the sensible answer for slate, flagstone and honed dark stone that looks flat when dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is permanence. The enhancement is in the stone, not on it, and it cannot be reversed without professional resurfacing. Applied unevenly it stays uneven, and applied to a stone that did not suit it, it stays that way too. Testing on an offcut or in an area that will be hidden by furniture is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            They also behave differently from ordinary impregnators on application. The resin content means excess is far more visible if it dries on the surface, and the buff at the end of the dwell has to be thorough and prompt. The same discipline described in our guide to <a href='/how-to-seal-granite-countertops/'>sealing granite countertops</a> applies, only with less margin for error.
          </p>

          <p>
            On exterior stone they are frequently the best available compromise: the wet look on a flagstone terrace, without the film that a patio's ground moisture would eventually lift. That combination is why they have largely replaced glossy topical sealers on outdoor natural stone in the trade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Switching From One to the Other
          </h2>

          <p>
            Going from topical to impregnating means removing the film completely. An impregnator cannot reach the pores through acrylic or urethane; it will sit on top, stay tacky and never cure. That means a chemical stripper appropriate to the film, a thorough neutralising rinse, and a full dry before any new product goes near the stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripping is more involved on textured stone than on polished. Slate, flagstone and tumbled travertine hold old film in every low point, and getting it all out usually takes two passes and a stiff brush. Missing patches show up immediately once the impregnator goes on, because those areas stay glossy while everything else is matte.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going the other way — topical over an existing impregnator — is the harder direction. The stone surface is now oil and water repellent, and film sealers need to wet out a surface to bond to it. Adhesion is usually patchy at best. If there is any chance you will want a gloss finish, decide before you apply anything penetrating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Like for like is straightforward in both directions. Impregnator over impregnator simply refreshes the pore lining with no build-up risk. Topical over topical bonds well provided the existing film is sound and clean, though each layer adds thickness and eventually the whole lot has to come off. That asymmetry is another quiet argument for penetrating chemistry wherever appearance is not the deciding factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever direction you are going, test the stone first to find out what is actually on it. Water that beads means something repellent is present. A drop of acetone on an inconspicuous spot that goes soft or cloudy means a film. Water beading with no acetone reaction means an impregnator, and that is the one case where you can simply clean and recoat without any removal at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Film on a Kitchen Countertop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Knives, hot pans, daily detergent and standing water destroy a topical film within months, and a scratched film cannot be repaired locally — the whole surface has to be stripped and redone. Countertops take impregnators, without exception.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Ground-Contact Stone With a Topical Product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patios, slabs and floors over concrete move vapour upwards continuously. A film blocks it, pressure builds underneath, and the sealer blisters and peels. If the stone touches the ground or a slab, it needs a vapour-permeable impregnator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Sealer to Prevent Etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Etching is acid chemically dulling the stone, not a stain sitting in the pores. No impregnator prevents it and no practical film survives long enough to help. Sealing prevents staining; coasters and prompt wiping prevent etching.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Slip Additive on a Topical Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glossy film on a stone floor, entryway or pool surround is genuinely hazardous when wet. Broadcast a slip additive into the final coat — it cannot be added afterwards without redoing the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying a Colour Enhancer Without Testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The enhancement is permanent and cannot be reversed without professional resurfacing, and an uneven application stays uneven. Test on an offcut or somewhere furniture will hide before committing a whole floor or countertop.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between impregnating and topical sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An impregnating sealer carries a repellent into the stone's pores and leaves nothing on the surface, so the stone looks unchanged and can still breathe. A topical sealer cures into a film on top, adding gloss and deepened colour but blocking vapour and wearing away under traffic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer is best for granite countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An impregnating sealer, specifically a fluoropolymer one rated for food contact. A topical film cannot survive knives, hot pans and daily detergent, and it cannot be repaired in one spot — a scratch means stripping and redoing the entire surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a topical sealer outdoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is usually a mistake. Exterior stone sits on ground that moves vapour upwards continuously, and a film blocks it until the pressure blisters and lifts the sealer. Outdoors, use an impregnator, or a colour-enhancing impregnator if you want the wet look.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do impregnating sealers change how stone looks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, when applied correctly. The stone should look identical afterwards. If it looks hazy or patchy, product dried on the surface instead of being buffed off at the end of the dwell — fixable with more sealer to soften the residue and a hard buff.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does each type last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Impregnators give three to five years indoors and two to five outdoors on most stone. Topical sealers give one to three years indoors and often only six to twenty-four months outdoors, because UV and traffic attack the film directly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put an impregnating sealer over a topical one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not successfully. The impregnator cannot reach the pores through the film, so it sits on top, stays tacky and never cures. The old film has to be chemically stripped, rinsed and fully dried first, which on textured stone usually takes two passes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What about colour-enhancing sealers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are impregnators with added resin that deepens the stone the way water does. They stay vapour permeable, which makes them the sensible way to get a wet look on exterior slate or flagstone. The effect is permanent, so test on an offcut before committing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

